There is [`move_cursor()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/3ad46cdb7a51281e0024416023b56cf5c7909ae7/doc/mini-ai.txt#L586-L596). It is also used for [`goto_xxx` mappings](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/3ad46cdb7a51281e0024416023b56cf5c7909ae7/doc/mini-ai.txt#L441-L443). For example, `g])` goes to the right `)`. With `'cover'` search method (either global or set directly in `MiniAi.move_cursor()` call inside custom mappings) it will not go to the next `)` textobject which can be used to imitate `%`.

Alternatively, there is a slightly more low-level [`find_textobject()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/3ad46cdb7a51281e0024416023b56cf5c7909ae7/doc/mini-ai.txt#L560) which can be used to decide where to put cursor.

A more `%`-like alternative is [`find` or `find_left` actions](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/3ad46cdb7a51281e0024416023b56cf5c7909ae7/doc/mini-surround.txt#L504-L505) from 'mini.surround'. Pressing `sf)` multiple times will act like `%` when traversing expression in parenthesis. Note, that it will possibly traverse through four cursor positions of surrounding (left start - left end - right start - right end).

---

The problem with `if-else` case in particular is that it 1) needs to be a separate textobject/surrounding; 2) that textobject/surrounding needs to start at `if` and end at `else`. Creating this textobject/surrounding robustly should be possible via [tree-sitter-based textobject](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/3ad46cdb7a51281e0024416023b56cf5c7909ae7/doc/mini-ai.txt#L707) and possibly some custom queries. I am afraid this is as far as there can be given an advice, short of actually writing the queries.

A **much** less accurate approach is to use the rule "any text from `if` to the next `else`". So something like this:

```lua
require('mini.ai').setup({
  custom_textobjects = {
    o = { 'if.-else', '^if().*()else$' },
  },
})

require('mini.surround').setup({
  custom_surroundings = {
    o = {
      input = { 'if.-else', '^if().*()else$' },
      output = { left = 'if', right = 'else' },
    },
  },
})
```

Hope this helps.

